/*****************************************************************************
    jitXnest -- DOES THE genKantParse SEQUENCE TEMPLATE SURVIVE NESTING?
    2026-08-08. SEQ 41 step 5. The campaign consequence of E2, measured.

    jitXtemplate certified the template ONE LEVEL DEEP and is green, but its
    two term-callees end in TAIL returns -- the one position where E2 does not
    bite. A real grammar does not look like that: in a rule fleet a term is
    ITSELF a generated rule wearing the same template, so it carries MID-BODY
    returns. jitXe2 measured that a mid-body return inside an INLINED callee is
    IGNORED: the early exit is not taken, the tail runs, the answer is wrong,
    and the run exits 0.

    So the question here is the campaign's, not the JIT's: is E2 a PREREQUISITE
    for genKantParse v1, or a nice-to-have?

    The shape is two levels of the template. The inner rule fails at its first
    term, so its early return must fire; the outer must then see zero and take
    its own early return. Both levels exercise the construct.

    Two tail flags, two channels, both needed: a returned zero alone cannot
    distinguish both-early-returns-taken from everything-ran-and-the-last-value
    was zero.

    Fire 1 wants out 0 with both tails 0. Fire 2 flips the inner term to succeed
    and wants out 1 with tails 999 and 888, so neither a folded constant nor a
    default satisfies both. Any disagreement with the interpreted oracle means
    the template does not survive nesting and E2 gates the campaign.

    ⚠ HEADER REWRITTEN 2026-08-08 AND THE REASON IS THE FINDING. The first
    version of this comment block CRASHED THE PARSER -- exit 138, zero bytes of
    output, before the first statement. Bisected to the header alone: the
    fixture body is fine and runs. Comments are not inert in this language
    (there is no lexer), and that block contained indented code-shaped lines
    including an arrow token. Prose only in fixture headers from here.

    ORDER: jitted half first (bear-trap #25), oracle last. Sentinel (H2).
    Run under a wall-clock cap (H5).
*****************************************************************************/
Start();
include(unitTests);
include(generate);
include(utilities);

search reset stack Grokking;
search Generating UnitTests bcOPs Utilities list;

register(JitXnest);
define
    xnInnerOK;
    xnInnerTail;
    xnOuterTail;
    xnSuk;
    xnOut;
    xnTermA code={
        return xnInnerOK;
        };
    xnInner code={
        xnSuk = xnTermA();
        if xnSuk == 0;
            return 0;
        xnInnerTail = 999;
        return 1;
        };
    xnOuter code={
        xnSuk = xnInner();
        if xnSuk == 0;
            return 0;
        xnOuterTail = 888;
        return 1;
        };
    xnDrive code={
        xnOut = xnOuter();
        };
    ;

xnInnerOK   = 0;
xnInnerTail = 0;
xnOuterTail = 0;
xnOut       = 7;
print "XN fire 1 setup : xnInnerOK =" xnInnerOK " (inner term FAILS -- both early returns must fire)":;
testing(xnDrive);
print "XN fire 1 result: xnOut =" xnOut " (want 0) innerTail =" xnInnerTail " (want 0) outerTail =" xnOuterTail " (want 0)":;

xnInnerOK   = 1;
xnInnerTail = 0;
xnOuterTail = 0;
xnOut       = 7;
print "XN fire 2 setup : xnInnerOK =" xnInnerOK " (inner term SUCCEEDS, recompile: NONE)":;
jitRefire();
print "XN fire 2 result: xnOut =" xnOut " (want 1) innerTail =" xnInnerTail " (want 999) outerTail =" xnOuterTail " (want 888)":;

xnInnerOK   = 0;
xnInnerTail = 0;
xnOuterTail = 0;
xnOut       = 7;
xnDrive();
print "XN interpreted  : xnOut =" xnOut " (oracle) innerTail =" xnInnerTail " (oracle -- want 0) outerTail =" xnOuterTail " (oracle -- want 0)":;

print "XN SENTINEL -- run reached the end":;
stop();
